# Page Law

The Page Law is not a style guide. It is the documentation form of the
mathematics: a page is stable only when its local carriers glue around a
generated claim-site.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: Page Law for stable Infomaterial exposition;
* phase: prolegomena;
* generating sites: foundational vocabulary, phase map, mark, utterance,
  predication cell, valuation;
* downstream uses: every stable Book I-XI page and every refusal to promote a
  frontier note;
* Lean carriers: `Infomaterial.Mark`, `Infomaterial.Utterance`,
  `Infomaterial.PredicationCell`, `Infomaterial.Valuation`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: any page may cite them, but neither replaces the
  Lean carrier or the reader-facing descent;
* provenance: the root manual's Page Law;
* familiar comparison: documentation checklist, theorem statement, concept page
  template;
* reader closure: treat each bullet as an obligation, not a heading to fill;
* frontier status: there is no Lean checker for Page-Law compliance yet.

The governing sentence is:

> A page is a proof surface when its carriers glue; otherwise it is a note.

## Claim-Site

The page must say which mathematical address it carries. An address is not a
topic. The smallest example is predication: once predication is generated, there
is a site where charge can later live.

Checked:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    PredicationCell.seed u =
      ⟨u.token, u.claim, u.token⟩ :=
  rfl
```

A page about value before this site exists is not early insight. It is using a
later carrier without generation.

## Phase

The page must say what makes its words lawful. For Genesis pages, the lawful
words are extremely sparse:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    (Articulating.utter u).head = u :=
  rfl
```

For language pages, valuation becomes lawful only after predication:

```lean
example : Valuation = (PredicationCell -> Charge) :=
  rfl
```

This is why a page cannot be stable merely because it is correct in ordinary
mathematics. It must be correct at the phase where it is filed.

## Generating Sites

The page must name its upstream generators. A generating site is not background
reading. It is the prior carrier whose output the current page uses.

For example, a valuation page is generated by predication and charge. If it
cannot name predication cells as its base, the word "valuation" is floating.

## Downstream Uses

The page must say what later claims it supports. This prevents local exposition
from becoming isolated settlement. A theorem with no downstream route may still
be true, but the manual must explain why the reader should carry it forward.

## Lean Declarations

The page must name declarations that settle or witness the claim. Prose can
orient, but in this library settlement is carried by Lean where possible.

Checked examples are preferred because they force the exposition to meet the
current declarations rather than a remembered API.

## Blueprint Or Proof-Tree Nodes

The page must say whether proof-tree or Blueprint carriers exist. Their absence
is allowed; silence is not. A green graph node without Lean is drift. A Lean
theorem with no teaching surface is isolated settlement. A provenance note with
no claim-site is latent material.

## Provenance

The page must say why the claim entered the system. Provenance is not proof, but
without provenance the reader cannot tell whether a frontier pressure, an older
note, or a formal module motivated the page.

## Familiar Comparison

The page must name the older mathematical object used as a comparison, then
demote it. "Category", "order", "sheaf", "spacetime", and "physics" are not
forbidden. They are forbidden as sovereign explanations before their generated
carrier exists.

## Reader Closure

The page must say what work the reader must perform between panels. This is not
pedagogical decoration. It is the manual's way of exposing the gutter: the
transport the reader must check rather than silently assume.

## Frontier Status

The page must say what remains unsettled. If nothing remains unsettled locally,
say what stronger familiar theorem is not being claimed. If a carrier is
missing, name it. If a theorem is conditional, name the condition.

## The Drift Test

A page fails the Page Law when any of these happen:

* it uses a familiar comparison as if it were the generator;
* it names no Lean carrier for a settled claim;
* it hides a missing carrier behind evocative language;
* it cannot say what phase makes its vocabulary lawful;
* it gives examples that do not type-check;
* it marks frontier material as if it were already promoted.

That is why the Page Law belongs upstream. It is the reader's instrument for
detecting when the book has stopped doing generated mathematics.
